{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"premise":"The problem asks what single mechanism best explains why the nocturnal UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others, and the dominant cross-city modulator in the urban climatology literature is the urban canyon geometry / sky view factor, which controls longwave radiative cooling efficiency.","case":"SRC_001 selects anthropogenic heat as the single dominant cross-city modulator, but this is a weaker answer than sky view factor. The problem's first part is well explained by stored thermal mass release, yet the cross-city variation is best explained by differences in radiative cooling efficiency driven by urban geometry: denser, taller cities trap longwave radiation and suppress nighttime cooling far more than cities with similar anthropogenic emissions but different morphology. Empirically, nocturnal UHI intensity correlates most strongly with sky view factor / canyon geometry across cities, and anthropogenic heat is generally a secondary contributor whose fraction of the total nocturnal heat budget varies and is often small relative to stored heat release. By foregrounding anthropogenic heat as the single best modulator, SRC_001 misidentifies the dominant mechanism for the cross-city gap.","counterexample":[{"type":"idealized","description":"Two cities with identical population density and energy consumption (hence identical anthropogenic heat flux) but different building heights and street widths: the city with deeper canyons / lower sky view factor has a substantially larger nocturnal UHI because longwave cooling is trapped, while the other cools more efficiently. Anthopogenic heat alone cannot explain the gap difference."}]}]}